[03:03:52.184] Browser LOG     [Candidate] Interview details fetched: {"candidate":{"createdAt":"2026-01-13T18:27:49.070428","email":"peleg@candidate.local","id":309,"name":"peleg"},"candidateId":309,"company":{"createdAt":"2025-11-29T18:48:03.177823","email":"shaniravia@gmail.com","id":1,"logoUrl":null,"name":"zacksventures","subscriptionTier":"starter","updatedAt":"2025-11-29T18:48:03.177825"},"companyId":1,"completedAt":null,"createdAt":"2026-01-13T18:27:49.092793","id":315,"interviewLinkToken":"feeaaaea-ce94-46b7-bb41-079e461100ba","interviewer":{"companyId":null,"createdAt":"2025-11-29T18:48:03.261075","email":"shaniravia@gmail.com","id":1,"lastLogin":null,"name":null,"role":"ADMIN"},"interviewerId":1,"language":null,"question":{"agentTemplateId":null,"aiCustomPrompt":null,"aiHelperName":"System Design Mentor","aiPromptTemplate":"helpful","category":"data_structures","companyId":1,"createdAt":"2026-01-13T15:02:03.585321","currentStep":7,"deactivated":false,"deactivatedAt":null,"description":"Build a log aggregator that can write, read, and count log entries.\n\nLevels: \"DEBUG\", \"INFO\", \"WARN\", \"ERROR\"\n\nExample:\n\n  Logger logger = new Logger();\n  \n  // Write logs (timestamp is auto-generated)\n  logger.write(\"INFO\", \"Server started\");      // t=1000\n  logger.write(\"ERROR\", \"Connection failed\");  // t=1001\n  logger.write(\"INFO\", \"Operation started\");   // t=1002\n  \n  // Read with filter (newest first)\n  LogFilter filter = new LogFilter();\n  filter.level = \"INFO\";\n  List<LogEntry> logs = logger.read(filter);\n  // Returns: [\n  //   {timestamp: 1002, level: \"INFO\", message: \"Operation started\"},\n  //   {timestamp: 1000, level: \"INFO\", message: \"Server started\"}\n  // ]\n  \n  // Count in time range\n  int count = logger.count(\"ERROR\", 1000, 1002);  // Returns: 1","difficulty":"medium","followUpQuestions":[{"answer":"\n1. inMemory:\n\n\"approach\": \"Store all logs in List, filter in code\",\n\n\"readComplexity\": \"O(n) - scan all logs, filter matches\",\n\n\"countComplexity\": \"O(n) - scan all logs, count matches\"\n\n2. memory with Level Index:\n\n\"approach\": \"Separate list per level: Map<String, List<LogEntry>>\",\n\n\"readComplexity\": \"O(k) - where k is logs with that level\",\n\n\"countComplexity\": \"O(k) - only scan relevant level\"\n\n3.fileBased:\n\n\"approach\": \"Separate file per level or time period\",\n\n\"readComplexity\": \"O(m) - where m is bytes to read from disk\",\n\n\"countComplexity\": \"Same, but with I/O overhead\"\n\n4.database:\n\n\"approach\": \"Indexed database with WHERE clauses\",\n\n\"readComplexity\": \"O(log n) with index - near instant\",\n\n\"countComplexity\": \"O(log n) - uses COUNT(*) with index\"","id":1740,"orderIndex":0,"question":"Walk me through the time complexity of your read() and count() methods"},{"answer":"Cache counts that don't depend on time filtering. Keep running totals that update on write()","id":1741,"orderIndex":0,"question":"How would you optimise count() if we only care about total counts (not time-filtered)?"},{"answer":"1. File-based, in-memory, or NoSQL database:\n\nData can become corrupted when multiple threads write simultaneously. The solution is to have all writes go to a queue, where a single writer thread processes them sequentially.\n\n2. SQL database:\n\nSQL databases handle thread safety automatically through built-in locking mechanisms.","id":1742,"orderIndex":0,"question":"If multiple threads call write() at the same time, what happens? if needed- give me a solution to the problem"},{"answer":"Each service still has its own logger. Logs are written to a standard output (or local file) in a consistent format.\n\nA collector picks up the logs, adds metadata like service name / instance / environment / requestId, and ships them to a central store.","id":1743,"orderIndex":0,"question":"If you have 10 microservices, how do you aggregate their logs into one place?"}],"generatedLanguagesJson":"{\"java\":{\"